<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="volatile esters biosynthesis (during fruit ripening)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6801" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The plant hormone ethylene plays an important role in the fruit ripening stages of climacteric fruits. It triggers the formation of complex sets of flavor and aroma volatiles at specific stages of fruit ripening |CITS: [9177311]|. In kiwi fruits, volatile esters are formed by the action of acyltransferases belonging to BAHD (benzyl alcohol-acetyl-, anthocyanin-O-hydroxy-cinnamoyl-, anthranilate-N-hydroxycinnamoyl/benzoyl-, deacetyl-vindoline) superfamily. The BAHD proteins use Coenzyme A (CoA)-thioesters as acyl donors, and one sub group of alcohol acyltransferases produce volatile esters that are associated with fruity aroma. The alcohol acylation of ripening specific acyltransferases was molecularly analyzed |CITS: [21450321]|. The fruit ester profiles of the kiwifruit cultivars reach peak levels at the soft end of their eating firmness stage |CITS: [9177311]|. Methylsulfanyl alkanoate and benzoate esters are part of the volatile profiles of kiwifruit |CITS: [20189207]|. |FRAME: ETOH| is the major alcohol detected in kiwi cultivars and nearly 50% of volatile esters are ethyl esters. &lt;b&gt;About this pathway&lt;/b&gt; The changes of volatile profiles in response to ethylene is well researched, and the above pathway documents the volatile ester formation during specific fruit ripening stages. The substrate preferences of several alcohol acyltransferases (AAT) of kiwifruit was analyzed and found to be variable. AT16 possessed benzoyl-CoA:alcohol O-acyltransferase activity and AT9 catalyzed mostly acetyl-CoA- and propanoyl-CoA- alcohol O-acyltransferase activities. Although ethanol is the main alcohol in ripe kiwifruit, AT16 and AT9 were more reactive &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; with |FRAME: BUTANOL| than with |FRAME: ETOH| |CITS: [21450321]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="xu" />
    <Author name="SRI" />
